Subject: [PATCH] sock_impl_linux: guard SO_NOSIGPIPE on the macro, not on __linux__

SO_NOSIGPIPE is a BSD/Darwin socket option. The file reaches for it under
`#ifndef __linux__`, which reads "if this is not Linux it must be a BSD" — and
that is false for every other POSIX-ish target. Emscripten is the one that
found it: musl-derived libc, no __linux__, no SO_NOSIGPIPE, so the file fails
to compile.

Testing for the macro is what the code actually means, and it is behaviour-
preserving everywhere the old guard was right: Linux does not define it and
skipped the block before, BSD and macOS do define it and still take it.

diff --git a/client/sock_impl_linux.c b/client/sock_impl_linux.c
index 71eedf8..5991a23 100644
--- a/client/sock_impl_linux.c
+++ b/client/sock_impl_linux.c
@@ -117,7 +117,7 @@ int native_connect_tcp_sock(void *addr, int addrlen){
 	setsockopt(sock, SOL_SOCKET, SO_SNDBUF, &sockopt, sizeof(sockopt));
 	setsockopt(sock, SOL_SOCKET, SO_RCVBUF, &sockopt, sizeof(sockopt));
 
-	#ifndef __linux__
+	#ifdef SO_NOSIGPIPE
 	sockopt = 1;
 	setsockopt(sock, SOL_SOCKET, SO_NOSIGPIPE, &sockopt, sizeof(sockopt));
 	#endif
@@ -138,7 +138,7 @@ int native_connect_tcp_sock(void *addr, int addrlen){
 	get_ret = getsockopt(sock, SOL_SOCKET, SO_RCVBUF, &sockopt, &opt_len);
 	LOG("%s: SO_RCVBUF is %d (0x%x)\n", __func__, sockopt, get_ret == -1 ? errno : 0);
 
-	#ifndef __linux__
+	#ifdef SO_NOSIGPIPE
 	opt_len = sizeof(sockopt);
 	sockopt = 0;
 	get_ret = getsockopt(sock, SOL_SOCKET, SO_NOSIGPIPE, &sockopt, &opt_len);
